<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miguel Primo de Rivera</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a>–<a href="/years/1930">1930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miguel Primo de Rivera was a Spanish general who seized power in a coup in September 1923 and ruled as dictator with the backing of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii">Alfonso XIII</a> until January 1930. His regime ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rif-war">Rif War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> with French help and invested in public works, but lost support amid economic trouble. His fall helped bring down the monarchy in 1931.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miguel-primo-de-rivera.jpg" alt="Miguel Primo de Rivera" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kaulak · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miguel_Primo_de_Rivera,_Kaulak_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Miguel Primo de Rivera was born on 8 January 1870 into a landowning military family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerez-de-la-frontera">Jerez de la Frontera</a>, the son of a retired colonel. His uncle Fernando was Captain General in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. He graduated from the General Military Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 1884 and served as a junior officer in the colonial wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. He watched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish-American War</a> in 1898. After fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> of the Rif he became convinced that Spain probably could not hold its North African colony and should withdraw if it could not dominate it. He was promoted to brigadier general in 1911 and later commanded the military regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, Madrid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s army suffered a heavy defeat at the Battle of Annual in 1921, and by 1923 deputies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> were calling for an investigation into the responsibility of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii">Alfonso XIII</a> and the army. On 13 September 1923 Primo de Rivera, as Captain General in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, led a military coup. The king named him prime minister. He suspended the constitution, dissolved the Cortes and set up a directorate of eight military men, and on 3 December 1925 replaced it with a civilian directory. His Patriotic Union, meant to be an apolitical party, failed to attract many members.</p><p>He withdrew Spanish forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, and when Moroccans attacked the French sector, French and Spanish united to crush the rebellion in 1925, with victory in 1927. His regime built dams and railways, and the Barcelona Metro opened in 1924. It organised 27 committees to mediate between employers and workers, and unemployment largely disappeared. It also censored the press, closed the Ateneo club and banned the CNT trade union. To suppress Catalan separatism it made the use of Catalan in church services illegal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The economic boom ended; the peseta fell and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> rose. He lost the support of the armed forces after he began to inject politics into promotions in the artillery corps, and the king drew away. On 26 January 1930 he asked the military leaders whether he still had their backing. Their cool answers persuaded him to resign two days later, and he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 16 March 1930.</p><p>The king, discredited by his support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>, went into exile on 14 April 1931 after republican candidates won in 41 provincial capitals, and the Second Republic followed. Primo de Rivera's son José Antonio later founded the Falange.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miguel Primo de Rivera” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 28 January 1930 the military dictatorship of General <b>Miguel Primo de Rivera</b>, in power since September 1923, was overthrown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Spain General <b>Miguel Primo de Rivera</b> governed through a Military Directorate from 1923 to 1925, Portugal came under military dictatorship in 1926, and in the 1930s Japan’s government fell increasingly under the influence of its army and navy officers.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Alfonso XIII backed <b>Miguel Primo de Rivera's</b> dictatorship from 1923 to 1930.</blockquote><a href="/state/restoration-spain">Restoration Spain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerez-de-la-frontera">Jerez de la Frontera</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.7,-6.12&amp;year=1909" aria-label="Jerez de la Frontera on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Miguel Primo de Rivera"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4581 4417h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4581 4417h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.7,-6.12&amp;year=1909">What was here in 1909? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
